      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/steamboat_arizona",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In Navajo, the community is called Hóyééʼ, meaning “state of fright” because canyon ambushes once haunted the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/steamboat_arizona",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Since the chapter house was built in 1986, Steamboat has operated as a Navajo Nation chapter with a president and council.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/steamboat_arizona",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Steamboat sits on the high desert of the Colorado Plateau in northeastern Arizona.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steamboat%2C_Arizona",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community preserves a historic spring and a rock formation that hold deep cultural significance for the Navajo people.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
